body:
I found bug (I think) in Validator.
If I have forms builded like:

``` php
foreach($user->contacts as $key => $contact) {
   $path = 'contacts.'.$key;
   echo $this->Form->input($path.'.value']);
}
$path = 'contacts.'.($key+1);
echo $this->Form->input($path.'.value']);
```

and user has 2 contacts (id 3 and 6), then I get 3 form inputs, which two is associated with database contacts, and one is new.
In validation I have closure:

``` php
$validator->allowEmpty('value', function($context) {
    return $context['providers']['entity']->isNew();
});
```

which is called 3 times, but value of $context['providers']['entity'] is interesting.
First two calls have properly entity in provider, but in third call entity becomes from second call.
First call - entity.id = 3, second call - entity.id = 6, but last call - entity.id = 6.
Because that, last call which is new, has entity from database, and isNew() method is false.

/Cake/Validation/Validator.php has method:

``` php
public function isEmptyAllowed($field, $newRecord) {
    $providers = $this->_providers;
    $data = [];
    $context = compact('data', 'newRecord', 'field', 'providers');
    return $this->_canBeEmpty($this->field($field), $context);
}
```

In $this->_providers attribute is stored not new entity. I think, in last closure call entity should be null or should be created as new.
Of course I have $context['newRecord'] property, but having old entity in context mislead me.

